WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.593
- Oh, my gosh, this is a great question.

00:00:02.593 --> 00:00:03.845
(inhales deeply)

00:00:03.845 --> 00:00:04.678
(groans)

00:00:04.678 --> 00:00:07.120
In that moment, I was like,
yep, this could be it.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.075
For many years, I felt outside
the loop on understanding.

00:00:11.075 --> 00:00:11.908
(claps)

00:00:11.908 --> 00:00:13.320
Let's get started. (hard exhale)

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.374
My favorite videos I've
ever worked on are...

00:00:16.374 --> 00:00:17.207
(camera flashes)

00:00:17.207 --> 00:00:19.100
Hey, everyone, I am sick.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.190
But I am dedicated to this Q + A,

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:24.640
because I have gotten literally thousands

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.140
of questions from you

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:29.010
and I'm going to answer
not every single one,

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.480
but a lot of them, today.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:31.500
Let's do this.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.219
- Can I ask you a question?

00:00:33.219 --> 00:00:36.342
(uptempo music)

00:00:36.342 --> 00:00:38.592
- Can I ask you a question?

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:42.441
- We have a delivery.

00:00:42.441 --> 00:00:44.427
- Just cause you, you really sound bad.

00:00:44.427 --> 00:00:49.000
- "Fire Cider," literally
has habanero peppers!

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.823
All right, (moans) fire cider.

00:00:52.173 --> 00:00:53.883
(gulps)

00:00:53.883 --> 00:00:55.240
(grunts on exhale)

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.720
Well, if that doesn't cure
me, I don't know what will.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:00:59.540
What's question number one? (sniffs)

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.440
What are the top three
countries I have visited,

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:04.660
ranked by cuisine?

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:08.453
Ooh, that's a good question. Um...

00:01:08.453 --> 00:01:09.640
(groans) I hate tops

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.500
because it always means I have
to, like, choose the best.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.700
Let me just give some
top three that aren't

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:15.670
THE top three.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:17.100
Taiwan,

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.040
Taiwan.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.700
Taiwan's street food is so amazing.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.540
It is optimized for convenience

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.430
and deliciousness and speed.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.540
And one of the things I
love about food is when

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:28.630
you order it and then
it comes moments later.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.040
And in Taiwan, that happens. It's amazing.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.110
Northern Italy, because I
haven't been to Southern Italy,

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.130
so I can't speak for Southern Italy.

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.740
But Northern Italy cuisine is excellent,

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.560
amazing, and very special.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.720
Number three, Northern Mexico.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.450
Northern Mexico near
Tijuana and Mexicali have

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.320
some of the best tacos

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:50.210
on the earth, in my opinion.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:53.240
Carne asada on tortillas de harina

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.330
that are so delicious.
That is my favorite.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:58.520
Do you ever unplug? How
do you manage to take care

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.710
of your mental well-being as a journalist?

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:02.755
I mean, clearly I'm not
doing great. (scoffs)

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:05.760
- Most of these people are
not ready to be unplugged.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.510
- I do unplug. This year,
I have been unplugging less

00:02:09.510 --> 00:02:13.110
because we are sort of in a
sprint in a lot of things.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:14.870
Bright Trip is sprinting, we're building,

00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:16.140
we're scaling Bright Trip.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.830
We're scaling my channel,

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:20.390
making a lot of content.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:21.560
So I have not been unplugging.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:23.240
And I'm sort of paying the price.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.770
♪ Hello darkness, my old friend ♪

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.910
In terms of unplugging from the news

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:30.130
and unplugging from global events,

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:31.620
I'm actually quite unplugged.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.310
A couple of years ago, I made a decision

00:02:33.310 --> 00:02:38.310
to not be constantly, deeply
involved in the news cycle.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.240
It was one of the best
decisions I made in my life.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.880
I follow the news, but I
don't follow the daily news.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:44.710
I read the news once a week

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:47.640
and it is "The Economist"
and other publications,

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.940
but I only let it come
into my life once a week.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.300
And I'm not following Twitter,

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:56.440
I'm not following any feeds,
Apple News, any of that.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:59.110
So that is a big part
of my mental wellbeing,

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:01.500
is being informed without having to feel

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.163
the constant buzz of, of daily news.

00:03:04.163 --> 00:03:04.996
(swish)

00:03:04.996 --> 00:03:07.790
What's the first effect in
After Effects that you learned,

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:09.500
and what made you want to learn it?

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:10.410
Oh, my gosh,

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:11.960
this is a great question.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:13.800
So, for those of you who don't know,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.750
After Effects is the
animation program I've used

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:19.970
since 2012 to make animations,

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.800
Maps, and motion graphics, all of it.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.170
The first effect that I ever
learned in After Effects was

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:27.450
trim paths.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.890
Trim paths is a way of drawing on a shape

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:33.640
so that it goes from not
being there to drawing on.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:36.825
And the first thing I
ever drew on was this owl.

00:03:36.825 --> 00:03:39.189
(swish)

00:03:39.189 --> 00:03:40.022
(owl hoots)

00:03:40.022 --> 00:03:42.150
This owl was the first
animation I ever made.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.530
The owl draws on and then it drops.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:48.940
And it's just my first
animation I've ever did.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:50.718
- Good job, little buddy.
(audience laughs)

00:03:50.718 --> 00:03:52.110
- Oh, man, you've got to start somewhere.

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:53.064
That was a good question.

00:03:53.064 --> 00:03:55.100
- Some honey...
- Bless your heart.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.490
- ...at the bottom so you need to stir it.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:57.533
- Bless your heart.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.330
Man, I am very well taken care of.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:04.410
Okay. Since Is is clearly
here, let's give Is a mic

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.440
and she's going to ask
the rest of the questions.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:07.610
- Look at me.
- Sure

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:08.443
- I'm the captain now.

00:04:08.443 --> 00:04:09.940
- [Is] What are the top three videos

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:12.290
you've worked on, ever?

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:16.600
- Ooh, man. How do I even
answer that question?

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:17.533
- You must choose,

00:04:18.456 --> 00:04:19.920
but choose wisely.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:23.160
- My favorite videos I've
ever worked on are...

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:25.130
I think this one I made a years ago

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:27.040
about why so many people go to Iceland,

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:28.258
like tourism in Iceland.

00:04:28.258 --> 00:04:30.140
(splutters)

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:31.770
- Why does everyone come to Iceland?

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:34.840
- That was the first
time that I had gone out

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:37.690
not as Vox or just not as anyone,

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:41.100
as me, to make an explainer
and to do it in the field.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.020
And it was really fun.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.403
We were sort of just going around Iceland

00:04:44.403 --> 00:04:47.480
just for this random
trip, and I just sort of

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:49.640
made this video about why
people went to Iceland.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.260
And there was this one moment

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:53.980
when I'm on this black sand
beach in Southern Iceland,

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:56.260
and I was like, what if I drew

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:00.450
the graph on the black
sand with a drone shot

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.620
and I just animate over the drone shot?

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:04.340
And it was this fun moment.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.430
And then I did, and it just worked out,

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:09.190
and then it was like,
we're in the blue lagoon

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:10.360
and there was no one there.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:12.637
And it was just like, just
the perfect little thing

00:05:12.637 --> 00:05:15.140
and the explanation came
together really nicely.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:18.400
The second one, I would say, is when I did

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.090
Hong Kong, China for, for "Borders"

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:22.600
and went into China

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.610
through, and we had to wait in the border.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:26.830
I had to go through this border patrol

00:05:26.830 --> 00:05:28.840
scene that took forever.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:29.890
And then I made it into China

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:31.440
and I just had this adventure in China.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.960
And that was a really
amazing experience for me.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.540
And it was a story that I was drinking up.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:38.720
I just, I couldn't get
enough of this story.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.300
Another "Borders" season one episode

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.960
that was a favorite of mine
is when I went to the island

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.620
of Svalbard up in north of Norway,

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.230
to this island that's
owned by 90 countries,

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:50.590
but it's controlled by Norway.

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:54.410
I went on a boat and went
to this, this village,

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:57.710
this Russian village on this island.

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:59.390
And it's Russia just has people there

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:01.390
just so that they can say
they have people there.

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:03.640
And they run this coal mining operation

00:06:03.640 --> 00:06:05.160
that loses money but it doesn't matter.

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.450
It's like, that was such a wild place.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:09.860
I was all alone in this, in this village.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:11.150
So those are some of my favorites.

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:12.230
Those were really good videos.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.930
- [Is] What motivated
you to go to therapy?

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:17.380
Did you have any initial resistance?

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:20.090
How has it impacted how
you show up as a partner,

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.720
father, and in your
relationship with yourself?

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:23.800
- Wow.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:24.633
- Wow.

00:06:24.633 --> 00:06:26.770
- I started going to therapy
a year and a half ago.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:30.290
I was resistant. I thought
going to therapy was

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:32.220
a thing that you did for six weeks

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:35.310
to sort of get fixed
and then you're better.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:36.870
And I sort of approached it in that way.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.460
After the first couple of
sessions, I was like, so

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:41.660
why did we just talk
for those two sessions?

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:43.360
Why didn't we get to the,

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:45.160
you know, the hard stuff.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:46.860
And I quickly realized that

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.630
therapy is actually coaching.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:51.210
It's like, it's like having a teacher

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:53.940
to teach you how to develop
new skills in your mind

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:55.090
and in your relationships.

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:57.250
Like playing piano or
learning a new language,

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:58.340
you have to practice.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.190
And usually having a teacher

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:02.240
helps you practice in the right way.

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:03.240
Over the past year and a half,

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:05.840
I've learned a lot about my mind,

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:07.840
about my insecurities,

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:10.180
about my behavior and where it comes from

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.150
and how it's linked to
other parts of my life.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.790
More importantly, I've
learned a lot about patterns,

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:16.540
patterns in my relationships

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:19.040
that are just cycles that just happen.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:21.550
And they happen unconsciously to me.

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:24.140
But as soon as you are with
a trained professional,

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.260
talking about your relationship,

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:26.740
you see those patterns and you're like,

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:28.690
oh, these patterns aren't actually

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:30.070
what I want in my relationship.

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:31.700
And here's how to change them.

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:32.710
That's a hard process,

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:35.030
it takes a long time, like any new skill,

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:37.990
but, man, it is, has changed my life.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:39.160
It's changed the way I think,

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.380
it's changed my relationship with Is,

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:43.160
it's changed my relationship with my boys,

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:44.680
it's changed my relationship
with my parents,

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:45.850
it has changed all my relationships.

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:46.860
And of course it has.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.090
I have new skills that
I didn't have before.

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:51.180
I wish therapy was more accessible

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:53.670
and more available to people

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:55.200
than it is in this country, at least.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.780
I'm glad that this
conversation is normalizing it

00:07:57.780 --> 00:07:59.900
a little bit more and
I'm grateful for therapy.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:00.766
Good question.

00:08:00.766 --> 00:08:02.180
(slurps tea)

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:04.570
- [Is] Have you ever felt
like your life was in danger

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:07.148
during a visit in a foreign country?

00:08:07.148 --> 00:08:09.306
(dial-up sounds)

00:08:09.306 --> 00:08:10.350
(ding)
- Yes.

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:13.320
So in 2010, I was hiking up a volcano

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:14.850
in Guatemala with my little brother

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:19.530
and a guy popped out of
the bushes with a gun

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:21.380
and pointed it at my head.

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:24.080
In that moment, I was like,
yep, this could be it.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:26.270
He just wanted to steal our stuff.

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:28.100
I don't think there was any scenario

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:29.730
in which he would
actually pull the trigger.

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:31.060
And I'm sure that gun wasn't even loaded

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:31.893
now that I see it now.

00:08:31.893 --> 00:08:34.200
But in that moment it felt very scary,

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:35.033
but that was about it.

00:08:35.033 --> 00:08:36.670
I think the other, the only other one was

00:08:36.670 --> 00:08:40.890
when I was driving up
these mountains in Nepal

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:44.360
on these really sketchy roads.

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:46.660
And there were just huge
drops left and right.

00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:47.720
Yeah, that's about it.

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:50.140
- [Is] What is one topic
you wish to explore

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:52.600
but you don't think you'll
be able to make a video on?

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:55.680
- A topic I really want to
explore is climate change.

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:57.780
And yet, every time I think about it

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:00.130
or every time I hear
about it or read about it,

00:09:00.130 --> 00:09:02.170
there's this sort of, I think there's this

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:05.640
collective apathy that humans
have about climate change

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:08.850
because it's so incredibly invisible

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:10.160
until it's not.

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:12.743
It's so incredibly slow until it's not.

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:17.150
And it's so incredibly
widespread, the causes.

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:20.580
And, and, and so, I am always scheming on

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:23.240
how can I bring climate change
to life and make it real?

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:24.520
I don't know how to do that.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:26.520
Hopefully, someday I'll,
I'll crack the code on that

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:28.640
but I've been stumped time and time again

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:29.970
on how to make climate change,

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:33.040
which is a very real
dooming thing, come to life.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:34.680
Because right now it's a lot of data

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:36.060
and it's a lot of squishy,

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:37.700
extreme weather patterns,

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:39.930
and it's just stuff that, that is hard to,

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:41.410
to really communicate to a big audience,

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:42.303
but I'll get there.

00:09:42.303 --> 00:09:44.354
- [Is] Do you travel with your children?

00:09:44.354 --> 00:09:46.239
If yes, what is their favorite thing

00:09:46.239 --> 00:09:47.590
about traveling so much?

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:49.470
- I do travel with my children.

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:51.300
We've made it a point to
travel with our children

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:52.230
since the very beginning,

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:53.690
which is more complicated

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:55.640
and difficult than traveling
not with your children,

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:57.660
but incredibly gratifying.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:10:01.680
When we travel, the
boys like to ride bikes.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.370
We always try to find bikes.

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:04.860
So we'll bike through Amsterdam

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:06.760
or we'll bike through
Florence or whatever.

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:08.420
They love boats and water.

00:10:08.420 --> 00:10:09.960
And so, we always find a boat.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.430
So one of our favorite things to do

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:13.700
is rent a boat and just drive it

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:16.150
through a river or a lake somewhere.

00:10:16.150 --> 00:10:18.130
I think that it's a
common misconception that

00:10:18.130 --> 00:10:20.771
