00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:14.910
- [Is] What have you
learned about yourself

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:16.740
as you've grown your channel?

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:18.310
- Oh, man.

00:16:18.310 --> 00:16:20.100
I have learned huge things

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:21.620
about myself in the past year.

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:24.840
Number one is, I just
like telling stories.

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:29.580
♪ I'm the storyteller, and
my stories must be told ♪

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:32.065
- I just am the guy who wants
to just sit down and write,

00:16:32.065 --> 00:16:36.300
and animate, and look at
cuts, and play with music.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:38.630
Like, I am not grand strategist

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:41.290
when it comes to building the business,

00:16:41.290 --> 00:16:43.410
and Is really is that person.

00:16:43.410 --> 00:16:45.610
She knows how to think about

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:47.570
building and growing and strategizing.

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:49.710
She's the reason I've
hired everyone I've hired.

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:52.490
We now have a team of eight people

00:16:52.490 --> 00:16:53.970
working on the channel

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:55.580
and that's all because of Is.

00:16:55.580 --> 00:16:58.860
If it were just me, I would
still be making all the videos,

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:00.050
start to finish, myself

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:03.140
and I would be not growing a business.

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:05.090
And so, I've learned that
I love to tell stories,

00:17:05.090 --> 00:17:06.440
I will always be a part of that.

00:17:06.440 --> 00:17:09.550
I don't want anything to do
with the business side of it,

00:17:09.550 --> 00:17:11.170
as long as I can keep telling stories.

00:17:11.170 --> 00:17:13.873
- [Is] How did you find
your videography aesthetic

00:17:13.873 --> 00:17:15.770
and, and include animation in there?

00:17:15.770 --> 00:17:17.523
Like, why?

00:17:18.520 --> 00:17:20.120
- It comes from a lot of influences.

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:24.240
Number one was just my own
interest in visuals as a kid.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:26.180
I always would film things and

00:17:26.180 --> 00:17:29.840
bring it up on the VCR and
like, look at what I filmed,

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:32.940
and sort of enjoyed looking
at it and the art of it.

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:36.240
I think maps became a
huge part of my life,

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:38.120
just as a teenager, I
always looked at maps

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:40.360
and I think that paper map feel

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:42.240
made its way into my style

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:43.570
- Paper maps.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:48.530
- I think that the growth
of informal filmmaking,

00:17:48.530 --> 00:17:51.142
with the 5D Mark II back in 2008...

00:17:51.142 --> 00:17:52.018
(heartbeat)

00:17:52.018 --> 00:17:53.945
- (echoed whispering) 5D Mark II...

00:17:53.945 --> 00:17:57.610
- ...to Casey Neistat,
who really made his work

00:17:57.610 --> 00:17:59.363
look almost accidental and DIY.

00:18:01.147 --> 00:18:02.160
(crash)

00:18:02.160 --> 00:18:04.820
And then I think the
biggest, most important one,

00:18:04.820 --> 00:18:06.090
as it pertains to this channel,

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:08.000
is what I learned at Vox,

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:09.770
specifically from Joss Fong,

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:12.120
about visual explanation.

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:14.130
When you say something, you show it.

00:18:14.130 --> 00:18:16.510
And you show it and the writing pairs with

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:17.597
what the visual is.

00:18:17.597 --> 00:18:19.170
And that, that seems so simple,

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:20.830
but the way that she taught me

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:22.870
and trained me to, to write in that way

00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:25.500
absolutely is the foundation of my,

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:27.020
of my style and my work

00:18:27.020 --> 00:18:28.460
and the value of my work, I would say.

00:18:28.460 --> 00:18:31.370
If I didn't have that training from Joss,

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:33.410
I probably would still just
be making sort of abstract,

00:18:33.410 --> 00:18:36.350
beautiful looking images
and abstract films without

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:38.430
any explanation and
journalism baked into it.

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:40.410
So thank you, Joss, as always.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:42.520
- [Is] How do you keep up your curiosity?

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:45.660
Are you afraid of burning out
and how do you combat that?

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:46.790
- This is a hard one to answer

00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:49.350
because I don't know how
I keep up my curiosity.

00:18:49.350 --> 00:18:51.368
I'm just a curious person, you know,

00:18:51.368 --> 00:18:53.580
I've just like, it'd be like,

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:55.620
how do you keep up your
love for chocolate?

00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:56.950
It's like, I don't know.

00:18:56.950 --> 00:18:58.357
I just like chocolate.

00:18:58.357 --> 00:18:59.310
(tense music)

00:18:59.310 --> 00:19:02.300
- Smells chocolatey, eh?

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:04.050
- It's, that's how I feel about curiosity

00:19:04.050 --> 00:19:06.040
is, it's just a deep preference of mine

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:07.640
to be informed about the world

00:19:07.640 --> 00:19:08.790
and to do what it takes.

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:11.940
I think that one important
aspect of my curiosity

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:13.570
and my desire to explain things

00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:16.250
is that for many years
I felt outside the loop

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:19.830
on understanding, and I felt dumb

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:22.950
and, and unintelligent
because the school system

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:24.060
sort of told me I was.

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:27.750
And so, I think a major thing
that drives my curiosity is

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:30.120
I want to make work that is

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:32.620
how I wish someone had
explained it to me, you know?

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:36.320
And so, that drives a lot of
my, of the work that I do.

00:19:36.320 --> 00:19:38.230
But in terms of my
curiosity, I don't know.

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:39.403
Like, why do you like
chocolate? I don't know.

00:19:39.403 --> 00:19:42.000
It's like a, it's a deep part
of me, deep part of who I am.

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.120
And yes, I'm, I'm always worried

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:46.140
that it's not going to go away,
but I don't think it will.

00:19:46.140 --> 00:19:47.976
- [Is] What is your
favorite item of clothing

00:19:47.976 --> 00:19:50.861
that is not the orange jacket?

00:19:50.861 --> 00:19:52.322
(camera clicking)

